Fungating Wounds
Palliative & End of Life
Exophytic cauliflower-like mass, Severe putrid malodor, Frequent spontaneous bleeding

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe spontaneous capillary bleeding and overwhelming malodor from a fungating breast wound causing extreme distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported spontaneous, frequent, and severe localized capillary bleeding explicitly triggered by a routine dressing change. The bleeding is continuous, and the wound is emitting a severe, overwhelming, and distinctly putrid malodor. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to achieve immediate hemostasis, manage the profound psychological and somatic distress, and adjust the topical wound care regimen to prevent hemorrhagic shock prior to the morning nursing visit.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The dressing stuck to the tumor, and when I pulled it, blood just started pouring out. The smell is making us both physically sick."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known exophytic, distinctly cauliflower-like mass explicitly protruding directly outward strictly from the cutaneous surface of the right breast. Over the past week, there has been ulcerative, crater-like, deeply necrotic tissue destruction explicitly extending directly into adjacent surrounding healthy skin. The wound produces copious, heavy, and continuously weeping purulent or strictly serosanguinous highly localized exudate. Tonight, the patient experienced severe localized capillary bleeding explicitly triggered precisely by a routine dressing change. The wound emits a severe, overwhelming, and distinctly putrid malodor directly secondary to profound localized anaerobic bacterial colonization. The patient reports pronounced, severe localized somatic pain distinctly radiating explicitly from the tumor site. There is profound psychological distress, severely altered body image, and resulting profound social isolation due to the visible wound and its odor.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but severely restricted by the bulky dressings and fear of sudden bleeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic hemorrhage; severe caregiver trauma from managing the bleeding and the odor.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is highly anxious, clutching a blood-soaked towel to the right chest, visibly distressed by the smell.
Physical Exam: Removal of the towel reveals an exophytic cauliflower-like mass with active, diffuse capillary oozing from the friable tissue. Surrounding distinct periwound skin explicitly presents with severe maceration, distinct erythema, and profound induration. There is visible, obvious macroscopic tissue necrosis. The odor is distinctly putrid and permeating the room. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the exophytic cauliflower-like mass, the severe putrid malodor, and the active frequent spontaneous bleeding observed during the exam.
Environmental Observations: The room lacks adequate ventilation. Discarded, soaked dressings are present in an unsealed bin, contributing heavily to the odor.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral analgesics were taken but are insufficient for the procedural pain of the dressing change.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fungating Wounds - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Hemorrhage and Malodor)
Differentials:

Systemic coagulopathy - Ruled out/unlikely as the bleeding is entirely localized to the friable tumor bed, with no petechiae, epistaxis, or hematuria observed elsewhere.

Acute cellulitis spreading to the chest wall - Ruled out/unlikely as the erythema is strictly confined to the immediate macerated periwound edge, without tracking lymphangitis or high systemic fever.

Arterial bleed - Ruled out/unlikely as the bleeding is a slow, continuous capillary ooze rather than a pulsatile arterial spurting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing complication of a terminal fungating wound: spontaneous capillary hemorrhage and overwhelming anaerobic malodor. The objective findings of a friable, oozing mass and significant tachycardia driven by anxiety confirm an acute crisis. Delaying medical care until the regular community nursing visit would result in dangerous continuous blood loss, unbearable pain, and profound psychological trauma for both the patient and the spouse. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to achieve chemical hemostasis using topical agents, prescribe topical antimicrobials to neutralize the odor, and provide procedural analgesia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied topical adrenaline (epinephrine) 1:1000 soaked gauze directly to the bleeding tumor bed with gentle, sustained pressure, achieving complete hemostasis within 10 minutes. Prescribed topical metronidazole gel to be applied directly to the wound bed to eradicate the anaerobic bacteria causing the malodor. Administered stat subcutaneous morphine for the acute somatic pain.
Positive Behavioral Change: When preparing for a dressing change (trigger), explicitly take the prescribed fast-acting pain medication 30 minutes beforehand and soak the old dressing with warm saline before removal (behavior). This prevents the dressing from ripping the fragile tissue, ensuring the change is pain-free and completely avoids triggering another bleeding episode (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community wound care specialist nurse for review of non-adherent dressing products tomorrow.
DME/Safety (Optional): Implement charcoal-impregnated secondary dressings to absorb odor.
Follow-up: Community nursing review in 12 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the bleeding restarts and does not stop after 15 minutes of firm, continuous pressure with a clean towel, or if the bleeding is pulsatile (spurting), the spouse must call 000 immediately for emergency transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The tumor tissue is inherently fragile and lacks normal healing capacity; occasional minor oozing is expected, but the goal is to prevent major bleeds and control the odor.
Clinical Course: The wound will continue to grow and produce exudate. Strict adherence to the new topical treatments and non-adherent dressings is mandatory.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Never pull a dry dressing off the wound. Always soak it completely first. Keep the prescribed topical bleeding medications readily accessible.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tumor blood vessels are very weak and tear easily. Pulling the dry dressing ripped these vessels. The adrenaline soak immediately clamped those vessels shut. The terrible smell is caused by bacteria living in the dead tissue; the new antibiotic gel will kill those specific bacteria and eliminate the odor.
Actionable Steps: Apply the metronidazole gel liberally with every dressing change. Use the charcoal pads on the outside to trap any remaining smells. Seal all used dressings in a ziplock bag before throwing them away.

